# The settings of the App project of the hosted deployment (Vercel, Postgres), as placeholders. The values live in
# Infisical, folder /app, environment prod (docs/deployment-vercel.md, section 3.1, has the table with every
# variable, who sets it and what the code does without it). Never put a real value in this file. For development on
# your machine the App has its own list: app/.env.example. The Dispatcher's: dispatcher/.env.example.
#
# Required: APP_SECRET, BETTER_AUTH_URL, DATABASE_URL, RUNNER_TOKEN and CRON_SECRET. Everything else is optional.
# A new instance is set up in the browser: run `pnpm setup-code` against the deployment's database, enter the code
# it prints and answer three questions (single or multi tenant, who may sign up, the admin account).

# --- The instance ---

# The address people use to reach the App, without a path: its production domain, https. GitHub sends people back
# to <this>/api/auth/callback/github, the "Callback URL" of the GitHub App (the one-click setup in the admin portal
# registers it for you). The App refuses to start without it.
# BETTER_AUTH_URL=https://reviews.example.com

# The master key of the instance: it signs the session cookie and encrypts the credentials stored in the database.
# At least 32 characters, random, never reused between environments. Create one with: openssl rand -base64 32
# Keep a copy that is NOT in the database backups: a database restored without its APP_SECRET loses every stored
# credential, and the secret cannot be recovered. Changing it signs everybody out. The App refuses to start without
# it and names this variable.
APP_SECRET=

# Deprecated: the old name of APP_SECRET. The App uses it as APP_SECRET when APP_SECRET is empty (and logs once
# that it is deprecated). A new instance never sets it.
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=

# --- Database ---

# The App role's Postgres URL (postgres:// or postgresql://): a direct or session-mode connection, never a
# transaction pooler. Nothing ever logs the password of the URL.
# DATABASE_URL=
#
# The most connections the pool of one function instance opens (1 to 20). Default: 3.
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX=3
#
# Which product this deployment is (code-review-com, the default, or factory) and which capabilities it has (a comma list,
# see docs/deployment-vercel.md section 3.1). Unset: the product's default, so the existing deployment sets nothing. A
# capability that is not in the set has no route, no navigation entry and no background work. Read at build and at start.
# PRODUCT_ID=code-review-com
# PRODUCT_CAPABILITIES=review,dynamic-review
#
# The DIRECT Postgres URL of the migrate role that `pnpm migrate` and `pnpm setup-code` connect to. `pnpm migrate`
# applies the migrations behind a session advisory lock at deployment time, before the new version takes traffic.
# Required for those commands only; the App never reads it.
# DATABASE_URL_MIGRATE=

# --- Dispatcher and Workers ---

# Required. The dispatcher credential: what the Dispatcher claims jobs with. At least 32 characters, random. Create
# one with: openssl rand -hex 32. The same value as the Dispatcher's RUNNER_TOKEN.
RUNNER_TOKEN=

# Required. The secret with which the platform's cron calls /api/internal/repository-sync (the daily sync with
# GitHub) and /api/internal/dispatch-sweep (every 2 hours: lapsed leases, resends, the give-up, the cleanup). At
# least 32 characters: openssl rand -hex 32. Without it the routes refuse every caller.
# CRON_SECRET=

# Optional. Switches on the operator API (/api/operator/v1): a machine interface, protected by this bearer token,
# with which an external service reads workspaces and sets their entitlements (limits, key mode, suspension).
# Leave it out and the API does not exist (its paths answer 404). At least 32 characters, random, and different
# from RUNNER_TOKEN, APP_SECRET and BETTER_AUTH_SECRET. Create one with: openssl rand -hex 32. See
# contracts/operator-api.v1.json.
# OPERATOR_API_TOKEN=

# How the App learns the Dispatcher: `webhook` (the App sends a signed wake-up call to the Dispatcher after it queued
# a job; the deployment's mode, see app/src/dispatch/README.md) or `poll` (the default: nobody is woken, a queued job
# only waits; the first steps of the deployment guide run in it). Webhook mode needs RUNNER_WEBHOOK_URL (https) and
# RUNNER_WEBHOOK_SECRET: at least 32 characters, random, and different from every other secret (RUNNER_TOKEN,
# APP_SECRET, BETTER_AUTH_SECRET, OPERATOR_API_TOKEN, CRON_SECRET, ...). Create one with: openssl rand -hex 32.
# The Dispatcher gets the same key as DISPATCHER_SIGNING_KEY_ID / DISPATCHER_SIGNING_SECRET.
# RUNNER_DISPATCH=poll
# RUNNER_WEBHOOK_URL=
# RUNNER_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# RUNNER_WEBHOOK_KEY_ID=k1
# While rotating the key: the key being replaced (both or neither); the App signs with the current key only.
# RUNNER_WEBHOOK_SECRET_PREVIOUS=
# RUNNER_WEBHOOK_KEY_ID_PREVIOUS=
# The platform's deployment protection bypass token, when the Dispatcher project is protected.
# RUNNER_WEBHOOK_BYPASS_TOKEN=
# Webhook mode, the sweep (app/src/dispatch/README.md): a job that waited this many seconds (60 to 604800) without
# a claim fails with dispatch_unavailable. Default 3600 in webhook mode, off in poll mode; 0 turns it off.
# RUNNER_DISPATCH_GIVE_UP_SECONDS=3600
# Webhook mode: send no more wake-up calls than there are free slots below this cap (1 to 1000, the Dispatcher's
# DISPATCHER_MAX_RUNNING). Unset: the Dispatcher's own cap is the only limit.
# RUNNER_DISPATCH_MAX_RUNNING=

# --- LLM ---

# The LLM key is not an environment variable: every workspace enters its own key in the UI (Settings, LLM access),
# and the operator's key for `managed` workspaces is saved in the admin portal (LLM key); both are stored encrypted.
# The GitHub App is created or entered in the admin portal (Integrations) and stored encrypted too
# (docs/github-app.md).

# Optional. The model reviews use unless the review settings choose another: the OpenRouter name of the model, for
# example anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5 (the default; the Worker adds the openrouter/ prefix). A value that is not a
# model name is ignored with a start-up warning.
# LLM_MODEL=anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5
# Optional. The context window (in tokens) of a model PR-Agent does not know; leave it unset for known models. A
# whole number from 1000 to 10000000 (for example 163840); anything else is ignored with a start-up warning. Left
# empty on purpose: this is not a secret, but the name looks like one to the check that keeps values out of this
# file.
# LLM_MAX_TOKENS=

# Optional, default off. Set to 1 (or true, on) to run Dynamic reviews on OpenRouter and Anthropic through the native
# OpenCode path (#578, design #544, docs/dynamic-review.md "The native path of a job"): the claim carries the model
# entry and price of the catalog (models.dev) and the Worker starts the passthrough proxy. With it on, a model for
# the operator's key must be enabled with a confirmed price in the catalog. Anything else, or unset, keeps the path
# of today. Read at every claim.
# NATIVE_OPENCODE_PROVIDERS=

# --- Model catalog ---

# Optional. The model catalog (app/src/models-dev/README.md): the sweep reads the public catalog models.dev (prices,
# limits and capability flags of the models of the providers the pinned OpenCode can drive) into the database, so a
# new model needs no release. A failed or implausible fetch keeps the rows it has. MODELS_DEV_URL is where it is read
# from (default https://models.dev/api.json): point it at a mirror when needed.
# MODELS_DEV_URL=https://models.dev/api.json
#
# `off` (also `false`, `0`, `no`) switches the fetch off: no call is made, the catalog stays empty and nothing else
# changes. Anything else leaves it on.
# MODELS_DEV_FETCH=on

# --- Set by the platform ---

# VERCEL: set to 1 by the hosting platform itself; never set it by hand.

# --- Test stacks only ---

# Send the sign in to a fake GitHub instead of github.com, and the API calls to a fake GitHub API. The addresses are
# refused unless the flag is set too. Never set these in a real deployment.
# ALLOW_TEST_GITHUB_ENDPOINTS=true
# GITHUB_OAUTH_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4010
# GITHUB_API_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4010

# Check LLM keys (the "test key" button of the LLM access settings) at a fake LLM instead of the provider. The
# address is ignored unless the flag is set too. A key is sent to it, so never set these in a real deployment.
# ALLOW_TEST_LLM_ENDPOINTS=true
# LLM_API_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4011/api/v1

# Send the Stripe API calls of billing to a fake Stripe instead of Stripe (the e2e stack of the billing page,
# fakes/stripe). The address is ignored unless the flag is set too, and even then only plain http on a loopback
# address is accepted, because the API key is sent to it. Never set these in a real deployment. Billing is
# configured in the admin portal and in nothing else: these two are not settings.
# ALLOW_TEST_STRIPE_ENDPOINTS=true
# STRIPE_API_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4012

# Longer pauses (milliseconds, at most five of at most 10000) between the repeats of a wake-up to the Dispatcher
# inside the request that queued a job, for a test lane that starts its Dispatcher itself. Ignored unless the flag
# is set too; never set these in a real deployment.
# ALLOW_TEST_DISPATCH_TIMING=true
# TEST_DISPATCH_INLINE_RETRY_DELAYS_MS=2000,6000,10000
